-#### .onEmpty()
+#### .onEmpty(options?)
 
 Returns a promise that settles when the queue becomes empty.
 
 Can be called multiple times. Useful if you for example add additional items at a later time.
 
+Pass `{signal}` to cancel this wait. If the signal is already aborted, or aborts before the queue becomes empty, the promise rejects with `signal.reason`. Cancelling the wait does not cancel queued or running tasks, or affect other waiters.
+
 > [!NOTE]
 > The promise returned by `.onEmpty()` resolves **once** when the queue becomes empty. If you want to be notified every time the queue becomes empty, use the `empty` event instead: `queue.on('empty', () => {})`.
 
-#### .onIdle()
+#### .onIdle(options?)
 
 Returns a promise that settles when the queue becomes empty, and all promises have completed; `queue.size === 0 && queue.pending === 0`.
 
 The difference with `.onEmpty` is that `.onIdle` guarantees that all work from the queue has finished. `.onEmpty` merely signals that the queue is empty, but it could mean that some promises haven't completed yet.
 
+Pass `{signal}` to cancel this wait. If the signal is already aborted, or aborts before the queue becomes idle, the promise rejects with `signal.reason`. Cancelling the wait does not cancel queued or running tasks, or affect other waiters.
+
 > [!NOTE]
 > The promise returned by `.onIdle()` resolves **once** when the queue becomes idle. If you want to be notified every time the queue becomes idle, use the `idle` event instead: `queue.on('idle', () => {})`.
 
-#### .onPendingZero()
+#### .onPendingZero(options?)
 
 Returns a promise that settles when all currently running tasks have completed; `queue.pending === 0`.
 
 The difference with `.onIdle` is that `.onPendingZero` only waits for currently running tasks to finish, ignoring queued tasks. This is useful when you want to drain in-flight tasks before mutating shared state.
 
+Pass `{signal}` to cancel this wait. If the signal is already aborted, or aborts before pending tasks reach zero, the promise rejects with `signal.reason`. Cancelling the wait does not cancel queued or running tasks, or affect other waiters.
+
 ```js
 queue.pause();
 await queue.onPendingZero();
